---
term: Ecumenical charter
section: structure
order: 75
definition: A charter designed to hold the position of the [[highest human charter]], with an objective broad enough to include members who disagree about most other goods.
seeAlso:
  - highest-human-charter
  - generativity-gap
  - limit-of-arbitration
---
The highest human charter faces the Social Charter's motivating question in its hardest form. Its members share fewer interests than the members of any charter below it, so it can't adopt one group's idea of the good as its objective. An ecumenical charter instead pursues the conditions under which its members pursue their own goods.

Several designs can fill the position. One example comes from an earlier model charter. It starts from the claim that nobody can know the highest goods in advance. So its objective is the sustained capacity to discover new forms of value, which members recognize only after they appear. It treats two failure modes as limits:

- Too little order, which destroys existing structures and loses accumulated progress
- Too much order, which eliminates novelty and leaves society rigid

The charter tries to keep society between these two limits instead of steering it toward a fixed destination.

Other designs are possible. For example, a charter could maximize its members' present [[welfare]] instead of the capacity to discover new value. The first design bets on what members want now, and the second bets on what they don't know yet that they want. The coalition that forms the highest human charter decides which design holds the position. Its members can amend or replace that design like any other charter.
